---
// "Server mode. Host it anywhere." The remote-dux pitch.
//
// PLACEMENT: after the office tour, before the FAQ and the install section that
// closes the page. The tour is where the reader meets the web UI; this is the
// immediate "so put that binary on a box that is not your laptop"
// follow-through, and it is the only section that asks the reader to think
// about deployment.
//
// LENGTH: this is a landing page, so the section states the offer, boxes the
// missing login, draws the topology and then hands off. The three detail cards
// it used to carry (the guards are not a login, one shared workspace, and the
// two things a proxy must get right) are not lost: every one of them is written
// out at length in /docs/server-mode and /docs/public-hosting, which is where a
// reader who is actually deploying needs them.
//
// THE SPINE IS THE MISSING LOGIN, NOT A FOOTNOTE.
//
// dux has no authentication of any kind. Not a weak one, not a default
// password: there is no auth layer. Every client that reaches the port shares
// one workspace and can drive any agent, browse the server's filesystem through
// the project picker, and read every session. So "host it anywhere" is only
// true as "host it behind something that authenticates", and that sentence is
// the section's second paragraph rather than a warning strip at the bottom.
//
// It is also, deliberately, part of the pitch. A competent reader deciding
// whether to put this on a VPS is better served by knowing exactly what they
// are signing up for than by a vague reassurance, and the honest version
// happens to be the more attractive one: the proxy is one Compose file and the
// docs page has it.
//
// The two built-in guards (the Host allowlist and the same-origin check) get
// their own card SAYING THEY ARE NOT ACCESS CONTROL. They stop a browser being
// tricked into talking to your dux; they do nothing about a person who visits
// the URL. Leaving them unmentioned invites the reader to discover them and
// mistake them for a gate, so the section names them and then disowns them.
//
// The topology is a real diagram, laid out by Graphviz at build time and
// emitted as inline SVG (see src/lib/graphviz.mjs). It was ASCII box drawing
// once, which read as terminal output when the subject is a deployment, and
// then a row of styled cards, which read as three cards. A deployment has
// nodes and edges, so it is drawn with nodes and edges. Every color comes from
// the site's tokens through the `.diagram` rules in global.css, the DOT below
// names classes and never colors, and nothing ships to the visitor but markup.
import Diagram from "./Diagram.astro";

const topology = `digraph server_mode {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  // Left to right, unlike the docs diagrams, which stack. This one sits in a
  // full-width homepage panel, so a vertical stack wastes the width and then
  // scales itself small to fit the height. The flow is also literally a journey
  // from outside to inside, which reads better along the page than down it.
  rankdir=LR;
  nodesep=0.30;
  ranksep=0.55;
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=13, margin="0.30,0.18", penwidth=1];
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, arrowsize=0.7, penwidth=1];

  client [class="d-outside", label=<you, anywhere<br/><font point-size="9">a browser, or a phone</font>>];

  subgraph cluster_box {
    label="a box that is not your laptop";
    fontname="Helvetica";
    fontsize=10;
    labeljust="l";
    style="rounded";
    margin=16;

    proxy [class="d-tls",  label=<reverse proxy<br/><font point-size="9">terminates TLS</font>>];
    gate  [class="d-gate", label=<oauth2&#45;proxy<br/><font point-size="9">decides who you are</font>>];
    dux   [class="d-app",  label=<dux server<br/><font point-size="9">claude &#183; codex &#183; git &#183; gh</font>>];

    proxy -> gate [label="  http"];
    gate  -> dux  [label="  http, private"];
  }

  client -> proxy [label="  https"];
}`;
---

<section class="py-16 sm:py-24">
  <div id="server-mode" class="anchor-offset container-app">
    {/* Full width: nothing sits to the right of this intro, so a cap would just leave a hole there. */}
    <div>
      <span class="pill">▸ server mode</span>
      <h2 class="heading mt-3 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        Server mode. Host it <span class="text-[color:var(--color-accent)]">anywhere</span>.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[color:var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        <span class="mono text-[color:var(--color-text)]">dux server</span>{" "}
        is the same binary that draws the terminal UI, serving the web UI
        instead. Which means the box it runs on does not have to be
        the box you are sitting at. Put it on a VPS, install your favourite
        agent CLIs and <span class="mono text-[color:var(--color-text)]">git</span>{" "}
        (plus <span class="mono text-[color:var(--color-text)]">gh</span> if you
        want the GitHub bits), and your agents run on a machine with a real
        uplink that does not go to sleep when you close the lid.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* The missing login is the spine of this section, not a footnote, so it
        gets a box of its own. The docs' admonition styling is scoped to
        `.prose-docs`, so this reuses the warning idiom already on this page
        rather than minting a third callout look. */}
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-warning)]/45 bg-[color:var(--color-warning)]/10 p-5">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-[color:var(--color-warning)]">▲</span>
        <p class="text-pretty leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-muted)]">
          Then comes the part everyone skips, so here it is first:{" "}
          <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">dux has no login.</strong>{" "}
          None. Not a weak one, not a default password, not a token you forgot to
          rotate. There is no authentication layer in dux at all, and none is
          planned, because the job belongs to the thing in front of it. So
          "host it anywhere" is really "host it behind something that
          authenticates you, over HTTPS". Do that and this is a genuinely lovely
          way to work. Skip it and you have published a shell.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <Diagram
        class="diagram--full"
        dot={topology}
        caption="Nothing installed on your side. Everything past the login has already been let in."
        label="A browser or phone reaches a reverse proxy over HTTPS; the proxy hands the request to oauth2-proxy, which passes it to dux server on a private network."
      />
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href="/docs/public-hosting" class="btn btn-primary">
        Put it behind oauth2-proxy →
      </a>
      <a href="/docs/server-mode" class="btn btn-ghost">
        Read the full server mode guide →
      </a>
      <a href="/docs/tailscale" class="btn btn-ghost">
        Reach it over Tailscale
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
